var e={level:`C2`,type:`transform`,questions:[{id:`c2-tr-001`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Despite' is followed by a noun or the -ing form: Despite being ill.`,original:`The fact that she was ill didn't stop her.`,keyword:`DESPITE`,template:`___ ill, she carried on.`,solutions:[`Despite being`]},{id:`c2-tr-002`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'Not until ...' at the start, use inversion: did I realise.`,original:`I didn't realise the danger until later.`,keyword:`NOT`,template:`Not until later ___ the danger.`,solutions:[`did I realise`]},{id:`c2-tr-003`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'No sooner had + subject + past participle … than' means 'as soon as': No sooner had I sat down than the phone rang.`,original:`As soon as he arrived, the problems started.`,keyword:`SOONER`,template:`No ___ than the problems started.`,solutions:[`sooner had he arrived`]},{id:`c2-tr-004`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Suggest' is followed by (that) + subject + base form, or by the -ing form: She suggested I apply.`,original:`'You should apply for the job,' she said to me.`,keyword:`SUGGESTED`,template:`She ___ for the job.`,solutions:[`suggested that I apply`,`suggested I apply`,`suggested that I should apply`,`suggested I should apply`,`suggested applying`,`suggested my applying`]},{id:`c2-tr-005`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A 'What' cleft sentence puts the important information at the end: What I need is a rest.`,original:`Her rudeness annoyed me most.`,keyword:`WHAT`,template:`___ her rudeness.`,solutions:[`What annoyed me most was`,`What annoyed me the most was`]},{id:`c2-tr-006`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'A bitter pill to swallow' is something unpleasant that you have to accept.`,original:`Selling the family house was a very hard thing for her to accept.`,keyword:`PILL`,template:`Selling the family house was a bitter ___ swallow.`,solutions:[`pill for her to`,`pill to`]},{id:`c2-tr-007`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`For a past action after 'is said', use the perfect passive infinitive: The house is said to have been sold.`,original:`People say that the castle was built in the twelfth century.`,keyword:`SAID`,template:`The castle ___ built in the twelfth century.`,solutions:[`is said to have been`]},{id:`c2-tr-008`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`In formal English 'Should + subject + base form' can replace 'If': Should you have any questions, contact us.`,original:`If you happen to need any help, just call me.`,keyword:`SHOULD`,template:`___ any help, just call me.`,solutions:[`Should you need`]},{id:`c2-tr-009`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Adjective + as + subject + verb' is a formal way to express contrast: Tired as she was, she kept working.`,original:`Although he is very rich, he lives modestly.`,keyword:`AS`,template:`Rich ___, he lives modestly.`,solutions:[`as he is`,`as he may be`]},{id:`c2-tr-010`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Hit the roof' means to become very angry suddenly.`,original:`The manager became extremely angry when he heard the news.`,keyword:`ROOF`,template:`The manager ___ when he heard the news.`,solutions:[`hit the roof`,`went through the roof`]},{id:`c2-tr-011`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'On no account' at the start, put the modal before the subject: On no account should you tell her.`,original:`You must not open this door at any time.`,keyword:`ACCOUNT`,template:`On ___ this door.`,solutions:[`no account must you open`,`no account should you open`]},{id:`c2-tr-012`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It's high time' + subject + past simple says that something should already be happening: It's high time you went to bed.`,original:`We should really start thinking about the future now.`,keyword:`HIGH`,template:`It's ___ thinking about the future.`,solutions:[`high time we started`,`high time that we started`]},{id:`c2-tr-013`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'There is no basis for' something means that no facts support it.`,original:`The rumours were completely untrue.`,keyword:`BASIS`,template:`There ___ the rumours.`,solutions:[`was no basis for`,`was absolutely no basis for`,`was no basis whatsoever for`,`was no basis at all for`]},{id:`c2-tr-014`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use 'It was + person + who/that' to emphasise who did something: It was Anna who found the key.`,original:`Tom broke the window, not Sam.`,keyword:`IT`,template:`___ broke the window, not Sam.`,solutions:[`It was Tom who`,`It was Tom that`]},{id:`c2-tr-015`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional links a past condition to a present result: If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now.`,original:`I didn't take the job, so I'm not living in Paris now.`,keyword:`WOULD`,template:`If I had taken the job, ___ in Paris now.`,solutions:[`I would be living`,`I'd be living`,`I would live`,`I'd live`]},{id:`c2-tr-016`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal English often uses 'be unable to' instead of 'cannot': We are unable to accept late entries.`,original:`We regret that we cannot refund your ticket.`,keyword:`UNABLE`,template:`We regret ___ your ticket.`,solutions:[`that we are unable to refund`,`we are unable to refund`,`being unable to refund`]},{id:`c2-tr-017`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Owing to' + noun phrase means 'because of': The match was cancelled owing to the rain.`,original:`The project failed because there was not enough funding.`,keyword:`OWING`,template:`The project failed ___ funding.`,solutions:[`owing to a lack of`,`owing to lack of`,`owing to insufficient`,`owing to a shortage of`,`owing to inadequate`]},{id:`c2-tr-018`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A cleft with 'The thing that ... is' puts the focus on what follows: The thing that bothers me is the noise.`,original:`The constant noise from the motorway is what bothers me about this flat.`,keyword:`THING`,template:`The ___ the constant noise from the motorway.`,solutions:[`thing that bothers me about this flat is`,`thing which bothers me about this flat is`,`thing bothering me about this flat is`]},{id:`c2-tr-019`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Negative adverbs like 'Hardly' at the start need inversion: Hardly had I left when it rained.`,original:`She had hardly sat down when the phone rang.`,keyword:`HARDLY`,template:`___ down when the phone rang.`,solutions:[`Hardly had she sat`]},{id:`c2-tr-020`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'But for + noun' means 'if it hadn't been for': But for the rain, we would have won.`,original:`If it hadn't been for your advice, I would have signed the contract.`,keyword:`BUT`,template:`___ advice, I would have signed the contract.`,solutions:[`But for your`]},{id:`c2-tr-021`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Notwithstanding' is a formal word for 'despite': Notwithstanding the cost, they bought it.`,original:`Although the evidence was strong, the jury acquitted him.`,keyword:`NOTWITHSTANDING`,template:`The jury acquitted him, ___ evidence.`,solutions:[`notwithstanding the strong`,`the strong evidence notwithstanding`]},{id:`c2-tr-022`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'The elephant in the room' is an obvious problem that people avoid discussing.`,original:`Everyone avoided mentioning the obvious problem of the budget deficit.`,keyword:`ELEPHANT`,template:`The budget deficit was the ___ room that everyone avoided mentioning.`,solutions:[`elephant in the`]},{id:`c2-tr-023`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Give something careful consideration' is a strong collocation meaning think about it carefully.`,original:`The committee carefully considered all the options.`,keyword:`CONSIDERATION`,template:`The committee gave all the options ___.`,solutions:[`careful consideration`,`very careful consideration`,`serious consideration`]},{id:`c2-tr-024`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It was only after ... that ...' emphasises a time: It was only after the launch that we noticed.`,original:`The editor only spotted the error after the book had been printed.`,keyword:`WAS`,template:`It ___ the editor spotted the error.`,solutions:[`was only after the book had been printed that`,`was not until after the book had been printed that`,`was only once the book had been printed that`]},{id:`c2-tr-025`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'At no point' at the start triggers inversion: At no point did she complain.`,original:`The public was not told the truth at any point.`,keyword:`POINT`,template:`At ___ the truth.`,solutions:[`no point was the public told`,`no point were the public told`]},{id:`c2-tr-026`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`In formal style, 'Had + subject (+ not) + past participle' replaces 'If ... had': Had I known, I would have come.`,original:`If the surgeon had not acted quickly, the patient would have died.`,keyword:`HAD`,template:`___ quickly, the patient would have died.`,solutions:[`Had the surgeon not acted`,`Had the surgeon failed to act`]},{id:`c2-tr-027`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Even though' adds strong contrast: She smiled even though she was sad.`,original:`He was exhausted, but he kept on running.`,keyword:`EVEN`,template:`He kept on running, ___ exhausted.`,solutions:[`even though he was`]},{id:`c2-tr-028`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Let the cat out of the bag' means reveal a secret by mistake: She let the cat out of the bag.`,original:`He revealed the secret about the surprise party by accident.`,keyword:`BAG`,template:`He let ___ about the surprise party.`,solutions:[`the cat out of the bag`]},{id:`c2-tr-029`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`We say 'do damage to' something, not 'make damage': The storm did damage to the roof.`,original:`The scandal badly harmed the senator's reputation.`,keyword:`DAMAGE`,template:`The scandal did ___ the senator's reputation.`,solutions:[`serious damage to`,`great damage to`,`considerable damage to`,`a lot of damage to`,`severe damage to`,`lasting damage to`]},{id:`c2-tr-030`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A 'What' cleft focuses on the object: What we need is time.`,original:`We need a clear strategy, not more money.`,keyword:`WHAT`,template:`___ a clear strategy, not more money.`,solutions:[`What we need is`,`What we really need is`]},{id:`c2-tr-031`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not only' at the start needs auxiliary inversion: Not only did she sing, but she also danced.`,original:`He not only wrote the script but also directed the film.`,keyword:`ONLY`,template:`Not ___ the script, but he also directed the film.`,solutions:[`only did he write`]},{id:`c2-tr-032`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'On condition that' means 'only if': You can go on condition that you call me.`,original:`You can borrow my car, but only if you fill it up afterwards.`,keyword:`CONDITION`,template:`You can borrow my car ___ fill it up afterwards.`,solutions:[`on condition that you`,`on the condition that you`,`on condition you`]},{id:`c2-tr-033`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Due to' is followed by a noun phrase: The game was cancelled due to the rain.`,original:`The museum closed because visitor numbers had fallen.`,keyword:`DUE`,template:`The museum closed ___ visitor numbers.`,solutions:[`due to a fall in`,`due to falling`,`due to the fall in`,`due to a drop in`,`due to declining`,`due to dwindling`]},{id:`c2-tr-034`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Wet behind the ears' means young and inexperienced: He's still wet behind the ears.`,original:`The new manager is very inexperienced.`,keyword:`EARS`,template:`The new manager is still wet ___.`,solutions:[`behind the ears`]},{id:`c2-tr-035`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Take heed of' is a formal collocation meaning pay attention to: Take heed of the warnings.`,original:`Nobody paid any attention to the warning signs.`,keyword:`HEED`,template:`Nobody took ___ the warning signs.`,solutions:[`any heed of`,`heed of`,`the slightest heed of`]},{id:`c2-tr-036`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'The reason (why) ... was that ...' highlights a cause: The reason why we left was that it rained.`,original:`The director cancelled the premiere because the lead actor fell ill.`,keyword:`REASON`,template:`The ___ the premiere was that the lead actor fell ill.`,solutions:[`reason the director cancelled`,`reason why the director cancelled`,`reason that the director cancelled`,`reason for which the director cancelled`]},{id:`c2-tr-037`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Nowhere' at the start is followed by inversion: Nowhere else can you see such views.`,original:`You won't find a more dedicated nurse anywhere.`,keyword:`NOWHERE`,template:`___ a more dedicated nurse.`,solutions:[`Nowhere will you find`,`Nowhere would you find`,`Nowhere are you going to find`,`Nowhere can you find`]},{id:`c2-tr-038`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Were + subject + to + verb' is a formal inverted conditional: Were he to resign, chaos would follow.`,original:`If the weather were to change, the expedition would be postponed.`,keyword:`WERE`,template:`___ change, the expedition would be postponed.`,solutions:[`Were the weather to`]},{id:`c2-tr-039`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'So as to / in order to + infinitive' expresses purpose: He whispered so as not to wake her.`,original:`She left early so that she wouldn't miss the last train.`,keyword:`AVOID`,template:`She left early ___ the last train.`,solutions:[`so as to avoid missing`,`in order to avoid missing`,`to avoid missing`]},{id:`c2-tr-040`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'A piece of cake' means something very easy: The test was a piece of cake.`,original:`Winning the contract was extremely easy for the firm.`,keyword:`CAKE`,template:`Winning the contract was ___ for the firm.`,solutions:[`a piece of cake`]},{id:`c2-tr-041`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Take exception to' means be offended by or object to: He took exception to the remark.`,original:`She strongly objected to being called naive.`,keyword:`EXCEPTION`,template:`She took ___ to being called naive.`,solutions:[`great exception`,`strong exception`,`exception`]},{id:`c2-tr-042`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`To focus on an action, use 'What sb did was (to) + verb': What he did was apologise.`,original:`She criticised the plan, but she didn't reject it outright.`,keyword:`DID`,template:`What she ___ the plan, not reject it outright.`,solutions:[`did was criticise`,`did was to criticise`,`did was criticize`,`did was to criticize`]},{id:`c2-tr-043`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Rarely' at the start requires inversion: Rarely have we seen such talent.`,original:`I have rarely heard such a moving speech.`,keyword:`RARELY`,template:`___ such a moving speech.`,solutions:[`Rarely have I heard`]},{id:`c2-tr-044`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional links a past condition to a present result: If I had studied, I would be a doctor now.`,original:`He didn't save any money, so he can't afford a holiday now.`,keyword:`SAVED`,template:`If he ___ money, he could afford a holiday now.`,solutions:[`had saved some`,`had saved any`,`had saved`]},{id:`c2-tr-045`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'While' can express contrast at the start of a sentence: While I like him, I don't trust him.`,original:`The film was praised by critics; however, it failed at the box office.`,keyword:`WHILE`,template:`___ by critics, the film failed at the box office.`,solutions:[`While it was praised`,`While praised`,`While it had been praised`]},{id:`c2-tr-046`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Put something on the back burner' means postpone it: We put the project on the back burner.`,original:`Their plans to expand abroad have been abandoned for now.`,keyword:`BACK`,template:`Their plans to expand abroad have been put on ___.`,solutions:[`the back burner`]},{id:`c2-tr-047`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`We say 'have an impact on' something: The law had a big impact on farmers.`,original:`The new policy had a huge effect on small businesses.`,keyword:`IMPACT`,template:`The new policy had ___ small businesses.`,solutions:[`a huge impact on`,`an enormous impact on`,`a massive impact on`,`a significant impact on`,`a major impact on`,`a profound impact on`]},{id:`c2-tr-048`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`An 'It was ... that' cleft can highlight a time phrase: It was in May that they met.`,original:`The scientist discovered the compound quite by accident in 1987.`,keyword:`IT`,template:`___ the scientist discovered the compound quite by accident.`,solutions:[`It was in 1987 that`,`It was in 1987 when`]},{id:`c2-tr-049`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Only when + clause' at the start inverts the main clause: Only when he left did I relax.`,original:`The truth emerged only when the documents were declassified.`,keyword:`WHEN`,template:`Only ___ the truth emerge.`,solutions:[`when the documents were declassified did`]},{id:`c2-tr-050`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'If it hadn't been for + noun' expresses a past condition that prevented a result: If it hadn't been for you, I'd have failed.`,original:`Without the volunteers' help, the festival would have been a disaster.`,keyword:`BEEN`,template:`___ the volunteers' help, the festival would have been a disaster.`,solutions:[`If it hadn't been for`,`If it had not been for`,`Had it not been for`]},{id:`c2-tr-051`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not only ... but ... as well' adds a second point; with 'Not only' first, invert: Not only was it late, but it was cold too.`,original:`The hotel was expensive, and the service was poor as well.`,keyword:`ONLY`,template:`Not ___ expensive, but the service was poor as well.`,solutions:[`only was the hotel`]},{id:`c2-tr-052`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Have butterflies in your stomach' means feel very nervous: I always get butterflies before exams.`,original:`She was extremely nervous before her audition.`,keyword:`BUTTERFLIES`,template:`She had ___ before her audition.`,solutions:[`butterflies in her stomach`,`butterflies in her tummy`,`real butterflies in her stomach`]},{id:`c2-tr-053`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'At someone's disposal' means available for them to use: The car is at your disposal.`,original:`The museum's founders wanted the collection to be accessible to everyone.`,keyword:`DISPOSAL`,template:`The founders wanted the collection to be at ___.`,solutions:[`everyone's disposal`,`the public's disposal`]},{id:`c2-tr-054`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'What I admire most is ...' puts the key idea at the end for emphasis.`,original:`I admire her courage more than anything else.`,keyword:`WHAT`,template:`___ is her courage.`,solutions:[`What I admire most about her`,`What I admire about her most`,`What I most admire about her`,`What I admire most`]},{id:`c2-tr-055`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Under no circumstances' at the start needs inversion: Under no circumstances should you lie.`,original:`Visitors must not touch the exhibits under any circumstances.`,keyword:`CIRCUMSTANCES`,template:`Under ___ the exhibits.`,solutions:[`no circumstances must visitors touch`,`no circumstances should visitors touch`,`no circumstances may visitors touch`,`no circumstances are visitors to touch`,`no circumstances can visitors touch`]},{id:`c2-tr-056`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Unless' means 'if ... not': unless it is repaired = if it is not repaired.`,original:`The bridge will collapse unless it is repaired soon.`,keyword:`IS`,template:`The bridge will collapse if ___ soon.`,solutions:[`it is not repaired`,`it isn't repaired`]},{id:`c2-tr-057`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'On account of' is a formal linker meaning 'because of': The road closed on account of snow.`,original:`We had to cancel the trip because of the storm.`,keyword:`ACCOUNT`,template:`We had to cancel the trip ___ the storm.`,solutions:[`on account of`]},{id:`c2-tr-058`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Sing the praises of' means praise enthusiastically: Everyone sang her praises.`,original:`The critics praised the novel enthusiastically.`,keyword:`PRAISES`,template:`The critics sang ___ the novel.`,solutions:[`the praises of`,`the novel's praises`]},{id:`c2-tr-059`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`We 'make' or 'achieve' a breakthrough: Scientists made a breakthrough in cancer research.`,original:`The research team made a major discovery last year.`,keyword:`BREAKTHROUGH`,template:`The research team ___ last year.`,solutions:[`made a major breakthrough`,`achieved a major breakthrough`,`made a significant breakthrough`]},{id:`c2-tr-060`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'The problem was that ...' introduces the key point as a clause: The problem was that nobody came.`,original:`Nobody told us that the venue had changed, which was the problem.`,keyword:`WAS`,template:`The problem ___ told us that the venue had changed.`,solutions:[`was that nobody`,`was that no one`,`was that no-one`]},{id:`c2-tr-061`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'No sooner had ... than ...' expresses immediate sequence: No sooner had we arrived than it rained.`,original:`As soon as the curtain rose, the audience fell silent.`,keyword:`NO`,template:`___ risen than the audience fell silent.`,solutions:[`No sooner had the curtain`]},{id:`c2-tr-062`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In case' means 'as a precaution against': Bring water in case you get thirsty.`,original:`Take an umbrella because it might rain later.`,keyword:`CASE`,template:`Take an umbrella ___ later.`,solutions:[`in case it rains`,`in case it should rain`,`in case it does rain`]},{id:`c2-tr-063`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Despite' takes a noun, -ing form or 'the fact that': Despite trying, he failed.`,original:`The team played well, but they still lost the final.`,keyword:`DESPITE`,template:`The team lost the final ___ well.`,solutions:[`despite playing`,`despite having played`,`despite the fact that they played`,`despite the fact that they had played`]},{id:`c2-tr-064`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Bite the bullet' means accept something unpleasant and face it: I bit the bullet and called the bank.`,original:`You should accept the unpleasant situation and deal with it bravely.`,keyword:`BULLET`,template:`You should bite ___ and deal with the situation.`,solutions:[`the bullet`]},{id:`c2-tr-065`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Decline to comment' is a formal collocation meaning refuse to say anything.`,original:`The minister refused to comment on the allegations.`,keyword:`DECLINED`,template:`The minister ___ on the allegations.`,solutions:[`declined to comment`,`declined to make any comment`,`declined to make a comment`]},{id:`c2-tr-066`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It is X that/who ...' contrasts one person with another: It is Anna who decides, not Ben.`,original:`You should ask the receptionist, not the manager.`,keyword:`IS`,template:`It ___ you should ask, not the manager.`,solutions:[`is the receptionist that`,`is the receptionist who`,`is the receptionist whom`]},{id:`c2-tr-067`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not for a moment' at the start triggers inversion: Not for a moment did I doubt her.`,original:`I didn't suspect for a moment that he was lying.`,keyword:`MOMENT`,template:`Not ___ that he was lying.`,solutions:[`for a moment did I suspect`,`for one moment did I suspect`]},{id:`c2-tr-068`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Should + subject + verb' is a formal way to say 'If ... happen to': Should you need help, ask.`,original:`If you should see Martha, please give her my regards.`,keyword:`SHOULD`,template:`___ Martha, please give her my regards.`,solutions:[`Should you see`,`Should you happen to see`]},{id:`c2-tr-069`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In spite of' is followed by a noun or -ing: In spite of the noise, she slept.`,original:`He studied hard; nevertheless, he failed the exam.`,keyword:`SPITE`,template:`He failed the exam ___ hard.`,solutions:[`in spite of studying`,`in spite of having studied`,`in spite of the fact that he studied`,`in spite of the fact that he had studied`]},{id:`c2-tr-070`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Hang by a thread' means be in a very risky, uncertain state: His career hung by a thread.`,original:`The negotiations are at a critical stage and could fail at any moment.`,keyword:`THREAD`,template:`The negotiations are hanging ___.`,solutions:[`by a thread`]},{id:`c2-tr-071`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Completely/entirely unfounded' is a natural collocation meaning not based on facts.`,original:`The rumours were completely without foundation.`,keyword:`UNFOUNDED`,template:`The rumours were ___.`,solutions:[`completely unfounded`,`totally unfounded`,`entirely unfounded`,`wholly unfounded`]},{id:`c2-tr-072`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It was not until ... that ...' stresses lateness: It was not until midnight that he arrived.`,original:`The government only acted when the protests turned violent.`,keyword:`UNTIL`,template:`It was ___ that the government acted.`,solutions:[`not until the protests turned violent`,`not until the protests had turned violent`]},{id:`c2-tr-073`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Seldom' at the start takes 'do/does/did' + subject: Seldom does he smile.`,original:`She seldom loses her composure in public.`,keyword:`SELDOM`,template:`___ her composure in public.`,solutions:[`Seldom does she lose`]},{id:`c2-tr-074`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Supposing (that)' introduces a hypothetical condition: Supposing you won, what would you buy?`,original:`Suppose the funding were withdrawn, what would happen to the research?`,keyword:`SUPPOSING`,template:`___ withdrawn, what would happen to the research?`,solutions:[`Supposing the funding were`,`Supposing the funding was`,`Supposing that the funding were`,`Supposing that the funding was`]},{id:`c2-tr-075`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Whereas' contrasts two facts: He loves cities, whereas she prefers the countryside.`,original:`Rents are rising quickly, and wages are not keeping up.`,keyword:`WHEREAS`,template:`Rents are rising quickly, ___ not keeping up.`,solutions:[`whereas wages are`]},{id:`c2-tr-076`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Lick someone's boots' means flatter them insincerely: He licks the director's boots.`,original:`He is always trying to impress the boss in an insincere way.`,keyword:`BOOTS`,template:`He is always licking ___.`,solutions:[`the boss's boots`,`his boss's boots`]},{id:`c2-tr-077`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`We 'make an effort' (not 'do an effort'): She made every effort to help.`,original:`The company is trying hard to reduce its carbon emissions.`,keyword:`EFFORT`,template:`The company is making ___ its carbon emissions.`,solutions:[`a concerted effort to reduce`,`a real effort to reduce`,`every effort to reduce`,`a great effort to reduce`,`a determined effort to reduce`,`a serious effort to reduce`]},{id:`c2-tr-078`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A 'What' cleft can name a cause: What made her leave was the salary.`,original:`He resigned because of the way they treated his staff.`,keyword:`WHAT`,template:`___ the way they treated his staff.`,solutions:[`What made him resign was`,`What caused him to resign was`,`What led him to resign was`,`What prompted him to resign was`]},{id:`c2-tr-079`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'So + adjective + be + subject + that' is an emphatic inversion: So loud was the music that I left.`,original:`The witness was so frightened that she could not speak.`,keyword:`SO`,template:`___ the witness that she could not speak.`,solutions:[`So frightened was`]},{id:`c2-tr-080`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A second conditional uses the past form for unreal present situations: If I were richer, I would travel.`,original:`I'm not as tall as my brother, so I can't reach the top shelf.`,keyword:`TALLER`,template:`If I ___, I could reach the top shelf.`,solutions:[`were taller`,`was taller`]},{id:`c2-tr-081`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In addition to' is followed by a noun or -ing form: In addition to singing, she acts.`,original:`The proposal is affordable, and it is also environmentally friendly.`,keyword:`ADDITION`,template:`In ___ affordable, the proposal is environmentally friendly.`,solutions:[`addition to being`]},{id:`c2-tr-082`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Bite the dust' informally means stop working or die: My laptop has bitten the dust.`,original:`I'm afraid the old printer has finally stopped working for good.`,keyword:`DUST`,template:`I'm afraid the old printer has finally bitten ___.`,solutions:[`the dust`]},{id:`c2-tr-083`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Put an end to' or 'bring something to an end' means stop it permanently.`,original:`Their argument finally ended the friendship.`,keyword:`END`,template:`Their argument finally ___ the friendship.`,solutions:[`put an end to`,`brought an end to`]},{id:`c2-tr-084`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'X is where ...' is a cleft that focuses on a place: Rome is where they met.`,original:`They live in a tiny village somewhere in the Pyrenees.`,keyword:`WHERE`,template:`A tiny village in the Pyrenees ___ live.`,solutions:[`is where they`]},{id:`c2-tr-085`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Little did + subject + verb' means the subject was unaware: Little did she know the truth.`,original:`We had no idea how serious the situation was.`,keyword:`LITTLE`,template:`___ how serious the situation was.`,solutions:[`Little did we know`,`Little did we realise`,`Little did we realize`,`Little did we suspect`,`Little did we imagine`]},{id:`c2-tr-086`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional can link an unreal present state to a past result: If he weren't so shy, he would have spoken.`,original:`She is a gifted pianist, so she was offered a place at the conservatory.`,keyword:`GIFTED`,template:`If she ___ pianist, she wouldn't have been offered a place at the conservatory.`,solutions:[`were not a gifted`,`was not a gifted`,`weren't a gifted`,`wasn't a gifted`]},{id:`c2-tr-087`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'As a consequence' introduces a result: He overslept; as a consequence, he missed the bus.`,original:`Prices went up, so demand fell sharply.`,keyword:`CONSEQUENCE`,template:`Prices went up and, ___ fell sharply.`,solutions:[`as a consequence, demand`,`in consequence, demand`,`as a consequence demand`]},{id:`c2-tr-088`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Put someone on the spot' means place them in a difficult position: Don't put me on the spot.`,original:`The journalist's question made the minister feel extremely uncomfortable.`,keyword:`SPOT`,template:`The journalist's question ___.`,solutions:[`put the minister on the spot`,`really put the minister on the spot`]},{id:`c2-tr-089`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not have the slightest intention of + -ing' strongly denies a plan: I haven't the slightest intention of going.`,original:`I have absolutely no intention of resigning.`,keyword:`SLIGHTEST`,template:`I don't have ___ resigning.`,solutions:[`the slightest intention of`,`the slightest intention of ever`]},{id:`c2-tr-090`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'X was when ...' focuses on a time: Monday was when it started.`,original:`I first noticed the leak on Tuesday morning.`,keyword:`WHEN`,template:`Tuesday morning ___ noticed the leak.`,solutions:[`was when I first`,`was when I`]},{id:`c2-tr-091`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In no way' at the start requires inversion: In no way will we accept this.`,original:`The company will not tolerate such behaviour again.`,keyword:`NO`,template:`In ___ such behaviour again.`,solutions:[`no way will the company tolerate`,`no way is the company going to tolerate`]},{id:`c2-tr-092`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Unless' = 'except if': You can't enter unless you have a pass.`,original:`You will only be admitted if you show your ticket.`,keyword:`UNLESS`,template:`You will not be admitted ___ ticket.`,solutions:[`unless you show your`,`unless you can show your`]},{id:`c2-tr-093`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'No matter what' means 'whatever': No matter what happens, stay calm.`,original:`Whatever you decide, I'll support you.`,keyword:`MATTER`,template:`No ___ decide, I'll support you.`,solutions:[`matter what you`]},{id:`c2-tr-094`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Back to the drawing board' means start a plan again after failure: The design failed, so it's back to the drawing board.`,original:`We have to start again from the very beginning.`,keyword:`DRAWING`,template:`We have to go back to the ___.`,solutions:[`drawing board`]},{id:`c2-tr-095`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Come under fire' means be strongly criticised: The minister came under fire from the press.`,original:`The plan was strongly criticised by environmental groups.`,keyword:`FIRE`,template:`The plan came ___ environmental groups.`,solutions:[`under fire from`,`under heavy fire from`,`under strong fire from`]},{id:`c2-tr-096`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`An 'It is ... that' cleft highlights the object: It is his tone that I dislike.`,original:`I can't stand his arrogance.`,keyword:`IT`,template:`___ I can't stand.`,solutions:[`It is his arrogance that`,`It's his arrogance that`,`It is his arrogance which`,`It's his arrogance which`]},{id:`c2-tr-097`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Never (before)' at the start is followed by inversion: Never before have I felt so tired.`,original:`The town has never before faced such a serious flood.`,keyword:`NEVER`,template:`___ such a serious flood.`,solutions:[`Never before has the town faced`,`Never has the town faced`,`Never before had the town faced`]},{id:`c2-tr-098`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Even if' means the result will happen regardless of the condition: Even if it snows, we'll go.`,original:`We'll go ahead with the picnic whatever the weather is like.`,keyword:`EVEN`,template:`We'll go ahead with the picnic ___ rains.`,solutions:[`even if it`]},{id:`c2-tr-099`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Given' means 'considering': Given his age, he did well.`,original:`She didn't reply to my email, which was strange considering how quickly she usually responds.`,keyword:`GIVEN`,template:`Her silence was strange, ___ quickly she usually responds.`,solutions:[`given how`,`given just how`]},{id:`c2-tr-100`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'As fit as a fiddle' means very healthy: At ninety, he's as fit as a fiddle.`,original:`My grandmother is very fit and healthy for her age.`,keyword:`FIDDLE`,template:`My grandmother is as fit ___ for her age.`,solutions:[`as a fiddle`]},{id:`c2-tr-101`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`We 'set' or 'establish' a precedent: The ruling set a precedent.`,original:`The judge's decision set an important example for future cases.`,keyword:`PRECEDENT`,template:`The judge's decision ___ for future cases.`,solutions:[`set an important precedent`,`established an important precedent`,`created an important precedent`]},{id:`c2-tr-102`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'All you need to do is (to) + verb' means that is the only action required: All you need to do is ask.`,original:`You only need to sign at the bottom of the form.`,keyword:`ALL`,template:`___ sign at the bottom of the form.`,solutions:[`All you need to do is`,`All you have to do is`,`All you need do is`,`All you need to do is to`,`All you have to do is to`]},{id:`c2-tr-103`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Only by + -ing' at the start inverts the main clause: Only by working hard will you succeed.`,original:`You can appreciate the scale of the cathedral only by standing inside it.`,keyword:`BY`,template:`Only ___ the scale of the cathedral.`,solutions:[`by standing inside it can you appreciate`,`by standing inside it will you appreciate`,`by standing inside the cathedral can you appreciate`]},{id:`c2-tr-104`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Provided (that)' means 'only if': I'll come provided (that) I'm free.`,original:`I'll lend you the book as long as you return it by Friday.`,keyword:`PROVIDED`,template:`I'll lend you the book ___ it by Friday.`,solutions:[`provided that you return`,`provided you return`,`provided that you'll return`]},{id:`c2-tr-105`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Despite / in spite of + noun' expresses contrast: Despite protests, the plan went ahead.`,original:`The law was passed, but many people objected to it.`,keyword:`OBJECTIONS`,template:`The law was passed ___ from many people.`,solutions:[`despite objections`,`in spite of objections`,`despite strong objections`,`notwithstanding objections`]},{id:`c2-tr-106`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'The tip of the iceberg' is a small visible part of a much bigger problem.`,original:`The investigation only revealed a small part of a much larger problem.`,keyword:`ICEBERG`,template:`What the investigation revealed was just the ___ iceberg.`,solutions:[`tip of the`]},{id:`c2-tr-107`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'A frank exchange of views' is a set phrase meaning an honest, often tense, discussion.`,original:`The two leaders had a very frank conversation.`,keyword:`EXCHANGE`,template:`The two leaders had a ___ views.`,solutions:[`frank exchange of`]},{id:`c2-tr-108`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'What makes X so ... is ...' highlights the cause of a quality: What makes it special is the light.`,original:`The author's use of silence makes the novel so haunting.`,keyword:`WHAT`,template:`___ the author's use of silence.`,solutions:[`What makes the novel so haunting is`]},{id:`c2-tr-109`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Only after + time' at the start needs inversion: Only after an hour did he call.`,original:`The rescue team only reached the climbers after three days.`,keyword:`AFTER`,template:`Only ___ the climbers.`,solutions:[`after three days did the rescue team reach`,`after three days did the rescue team manage to reach`]},{id:`c2-tr-110`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Without + noun' can replace an 'if' clause in the third conditional: Without your help, I would never have finished.`,original:`The only reason we found the cave was that a shepherd guided us.`,keyword:`NEVER`,template:`Without a shepherd to guide us, we ___ the cave.`,solutions:[`would never have found`,`would have never found`,`could never have found`,`might never have found`]},{id:`c2-tr-111`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Although' introduces a contrasting clause: It's cheap, although it's slow.`,original:`The software is powerful; on the other hand, it is hard to learn.`,keyword:`ALTHOUGH`,template:`The software is hard to learn, ___ powerful.`,solutions:[`although it is`,`although it's`]},{id:`c2-tr-112`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Don't count your chickens before they hatch' means don't rely on something that hasn't happened yet.`,original:`Don't count on the deal until it is actually signed.`,keyword:`CHICKENS`,template:`Don't count ___ they hatch; the deal isn't signed yet.`,solutions:[`your chickens before`]},{id:`c2-tr-113`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Painfully aware' means knowing something very clearly, often uncomfortably: He was painfully aware of his mistake.`,original:`She knows only too well the risks involved.`,keyword:`PAINFULLY`,template:`She is ___ the risks involved.`,solutions:[`painfully aware of`]},{id:`c2-tr-114`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`In an 'It is X who/that ...' cleft, the focused noun comes first: It is you who should apologise.`,original:`The landlord, rather than the tenants, should pay for the repairs.`,keyword:`WHO`,template:`It is the landlord ___ for the repairs, rather than the tenants.`,solutions:[`who should pay`,`who ought to pay`,`who must pay`]},{id:`c2-tr-115`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Such was the + noun + of X that ...' is an emphatic inversion: Such was her anger that she left.`,original:`The plot was so complex that few readers followed it.`,keyword:`SUCH`,template:`___ the plot that few readers followed it.`,solutions:[`Such was the complexity of`]},{id:`c2-tr-116`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Wish + past perfect' expresses regret about the past: I wish I had listened.`,original:`I regret not accepting their offer.`,keyword:`WISH`,template:`I ___ their offer.`,solutions:[`wish I had accepted`,`wish that I had accepted`,`wish I'd accepted`,`wish that I'd accepted`]},{id:`c2-tr-117`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'The result of + noun' links an outcome to its cause: The flood was the result of heavy rain.`,original:`He was promoted because he had worked hard for years.`,keyword:`RESULT`,template:`His promotion was the ___ years of hard work.`,solutions:[`result of`,`direct result of`]},{id:`c2-tr-118`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Be at loggerheads' means be in strong disagreement: The neighbours are at loggerheads.`,original:`The two companies are fierce rivals and always fighting.`,keyword:`LOGGERHEADS`,template:`The two companies are always at ___.`,solutions:[`loggerheads with each other`,`loggerheads with one another`,`loggerheads`]},{id:`c2-tr-119`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`We 'cast doubt on' something, meaning make it seem less certain: The data cast doubt on the theory.`,original:`The new evidence raised serious doubts about his guilt.`,keyword:`DOUBT`,template:`The new evidence cast ___ his guilt.`,solutions:[`serious doubt on`,`serious doubt upon`,`doubt on`,`doubt upon`,`grave doubt on`]},{id:`c2-tr-120`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`In a 'What' cleft, 'was' links the clause to the focus: What impressed me was her energy.`,original:`The coach's calm attitude impressed the players most of all.`,keyword:`WAS`,template:`What impressed the players most of all ___ attitude.`,solutions:[`was the coach's calm`]},{id:`c2-tr-121`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'nor' introducing a second negative, invert the auxiliary: She didn't call, nor did she write.`,original:`He didn't apologise, and he didn't even look embarrassed.`,keyword:`NOR`,template:`He didn't apologise, ___ embarrassed.`,solutions:[`nor did he even look`,`nor did he look`,`and nor did he even look`,`and nor did he look`]},{id:`c2-tr-122`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`The third conditional can put the 'if' clause second: I'd have missed it if you hadn't called.`,original:`If you hadn't reminded me, I would have forgotten the meeting.`,keyword:`REMINDED`,template:`I would have forgotten the meeting ___ me.`,solutions:[`if you hadn't reminded`,`if you had not reminded`,`had you not reminded`]},{id:`c2-tr-123`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Failing that' means 'if that is not possible': Ask a neighbour or, failing that, call me.`,original:`I'll call you tonight; otherwise I'll email you tomorrow.`,keyword:`FAILING`,template:`I'll call you tonight; ___ email you tomorrow.`,solutions:[`failing that, I'll`,`failing that, I will`,`failing that I'll`,`failing that I will`]},{id:`c2-tr-124`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'As if money grew on trees' describes spending carelessly, as though money were unlimited.`,original:`He spent his inheritance carelessly, as though his supply of cash were unlimited.`,keyword:`MONEY`,template:`He spent his inheritance as if ___ trees.`,solutions:[`money grew on`,`money grows on`]},{id:`c2-tr-125`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Come to grips with' means start dealing seriously with a problem.`,original:`The government must deal with the housing crisis immediately.`,keyword:`GRIPS`,template:`The government must come ___ the housing crisis immediately.`,solutions:[`to grips with`]},{id:`c2-tr-126`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Contrastive clefts can pair 'It wasn't X ...' with 'what ... was Y': It wasn't the cost; what worried me was the risk.`,original:`I didn't lose my temper because of his lateness; it was his excuses.`,keyword:`WHAT`,template:`It wasn't his lateness that made me lose my temper; ___ his excuses.`,solutions:[`what made me lose my temper was`,`what made me lose it was`]},{id:`c2-tr-127`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Only later' at the start triggers inversion: Only later did they understand.`,original:`I realised only later that I had left my passport at the hotel.`,keyword:`LATER`,template:`Only ___ that I had left my passport at the hotel.`,solutions:[`later did I realise`,`later did I realize`]},{id:`c2-tr-128`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'If only + past simple' expresses a strong wish about the present: If only I had more time.`,original:`It's a pity I don't speak Mandarin.`,keyword:`ONLY`,template:`If ___ Mandarin.`,solutions:[`only I spoke`,`only I could speak`,`only I were able to speak`,`only I was able to speak`]},{id:`c2-tr-129`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Adjective + as/though + subject + may be' is an emphatic concessive pattern: Tired as she may be, she won't stop.`,original:`Prices may be high, but the quality is outstanding.`,keyword:`MAY`,template:`High ___, the quality is outstanding.`,solutions:[`as prices may be`,`though prices may be`,`as the prices may be`,`though the prices may be`]},{id:`c2-tr-130`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Touch a (raw) nerve' means mention a sensitive subject that upsets someone.`,original:`Mentioning his ex-wife really upset him.`,keyword:`NERVE`,template:`Mentioning his ex-wife really touched ___.`,solutions:[`a nerve`,`a raw nerve`]},{id:`c2-tr-131`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Give something short shrift' means dismiss it quickly and without consideration.`,original:`Their proposal was completely rejected by the board.`,keyword:`SHORT`,template:`The board gave their proposal ___.`,solutions:[`short shrift`]},{id:`c2-tr-132`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'What they did was (to) + verb' focuses on the action: What they did was leave early.`,original:`They didn't so much argue as refuse to speak to each other.`,keyword:`DID`,template:`What they ___ refuse to speak to each other.`,solutions:[`did was`,`did was to`]},{id:`c2-tr-133`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not once' at the start requires inversion: Not once did he thank me.`,original:`The minister did not mention the scandal once during the interview.`,keyword:`ONCE`,template:`Not ___ the scandal during the interview.`,solutions:[`once did the minister mention`]},{id:`c2-tr-134`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In the event that' or 'in the event of + noun/-ing' means 'if': In the event of fire, use the stairs.`,original:`Should the alarm sound, leave the building immediately.`,keyword:`EVENT`,template:`In ___ sound, leave the building immediately.`,solutions:[`the event that the alarm should`]},{id:`c2-tr-135`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In order to + infinitive' or 'in order that + clause' expresses purpose: He saved in order to buy a car.`,original:`They delayed the launch so that they could fix the bugs.`,keyword:`ORDER`,template:`They delayed the launch ___ fix the bugs.`,solutions:[`in order to`,`in order that they could`,`in order that they might`]},{id:`c2-tr-136`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Over the moon' means extremely happy: They were over the moon with the news.`,original:`She's in a very good mood since she got the job.`,keyword:`MOON`,template:`She's been over ___ since she got the job.`,solutions:[`the moon`]},{id:`c2-tr-137`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Receive widespread/critical acclaim' means be praised very highly.`,original:`The novel received very positive reviews from the critics.`,keyword:`ACCLAIM`,template:`The novel received ___ from the critics.`,solutions:[`widespread acclaim`,`great acclaim`,`critical acclaim`,`universal acclaim`,`high acclaim`]},{id:`c2-tr-138`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It was X that ...' singles out one cause among others: It was the music that saved the film.`,original:`The sea air helped her recover, more than the medicine.`,keyword:`IT`,template:`___ helped her recover, more than the medicine.`,solutions:[`It was the sea air that`,`It was the sea air which`]},{id:`c2-tr-139`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Scarcely had ... when ...' means something happened immediately after: Scarcely had we eaten when the bell rang.`,original:`He had just finished his speech when the lights went out.`,keyword:`SCARCELY`,template:`___ his speech when the lights went out.`,solutions:[`Scarcely had he finished`]},{id:`c2-tr-140`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`An inverted mixed conditional: Had he not been so lazy, he would be rich now.`,original:`If you hadn't been so careless, we wouldn't be in this mess now.`,keyword:`CARELESS`,template:`Had ___, we wouldn't be in this mess now.`,solutions:[`you not been so careless`,`you been less careless`]},{id:`c2-tr-141`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'However + adjective/adverb' means 'no matter how': However fast you run, you won't catch him.`,original:`No matter how hard he tries, he can't please his father.`,keyword:`HOWEVER`,template:`___ tries, he can't please his father.`,solutions:[`However hard he`,`However much he`]},{id:`c2-tr-142`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not pull any punches' means speak frankly and directly: The report pulls no punches.`,original:`The lawyer explained the situation clearly and directly, without hiding anything.`,keyword:`PUNCHES`,template:`The lawyer didn't pull ___ when explaining the situation.`,solutions:[`any punches`,`her punches`,`his punches`]},{id:`c2-tr-143`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Have a bearing on' means have an influence on or be relevant to: Age has no bearing on this.`,original:`The decision was strongly influenced by public opinion.`,keyword:`BEARING`,template:`Public opinion had ___ the decision.`,solutions:[`a strong bearing on`,`a significant bearing on`,`a considerable bearing on`,`a great bearing on`,`a major bearing on`,`a direct bearing on`]},{id:`c2-tr-144`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A 'What' cleft can end in a noun phrase: What surprised us was the speed at which it grew.`,original:`We were not prepared for how quickly the river rose.`,keyword:`SPEED`,template:`What we were not prepared for ___ the river rose.`,solutions:[`was the speed at which`,`was the speed with which`]},{id:`c2-tr-145`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Never had + subject + past participle' is used for emphasis about the past: Never had I seen such crowds.`,original:`The orchestra had never played with such passion.`,keyword:`NEVER`,template:`___ with such passion.`,solutions:[`Never had the orchestra played`,`Never before had the orchestra played`]},{id:`c2-tr-146`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A second conditional describes an imaginary present or future situation: If you had wings, would you fly?`,original:`Imagine you had the chance to live abroad: would you take it?`,keyword:`CHANCE`,template:`If you ___ live abroad, would you take it?`,solutions:[`had the chance to`,`were given the chance to`,`got the chance to`,`had a chance to`]},{id:`c2-tr-147`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Hence' means 'for this reason' and can be followed by a noun phrase: It rained, hence the delay.`,original:`The concert was cancelled; as a result, refunds were offered.`,keyword:`HENCE`,template:`The concert was cancelled, and ___ offered.`,solutions:[`hence refunds were`]},{id:`c2-tr-148`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Play it by ear' means decide as things develop instead of planning: We'll play it by ear.`,original:`Let's not make fixed plans and just decide what to do as things develop.`,keyword:`EAR`,template:`Let's ___ and decide as things develop.`,solutions:[`play it by ear`]},{id:`c2-tr-149`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Extreme adjectives like 'ravenous' collocate with 'absolutely', not 'very': I'm absolutely ravenous.`,original:`The hikers were very hungry after the long walk.`,keyword:`RAVENOUS`,template:`The hikers were ___ after the long walk.`,solutions:[`absolutely ravenous`,`utterly ravenous`]},{id:`c2-tr-150`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It wasn't until ... that ...' marks the moment something finally happened: It wasn't until June that it rained.`,original:`I only realised how much I missed home when I moved abroad.`,keyword:`WASN'T`,template:`It ___ abroad that I realised how much I missed home.`,solutions:[`wasn't until I moved`,`wasn't until I had moved`]},{id:`c2-tr-151`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not until + clause' at the start inverts the main clause: Not until he apologised did she forgive him.`,original:`The farmers couldn't harvest anything until the rains stopped.`,keyword:`UNTIL`,template:`Not ___ harvest anything.`,solutions:[`until the rains stopped could the farmers`,`until the rains had stopped could the farmers`,`until the rains stopped were the farmers able to`,`until the rains had stopped were the farmers able to`]},{id:`c2-tr-152`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Regardless of whether' means the condition does not affect the result: We'll go regardless of whether it rains.`,original:`Whether or not the board approves, we are launching the product.`,keyword:`REGARDLESS`,template:`We are launching the product ___ the board approves.`,solutions:[`regardless of whether`,`regardless of whether or not`]},{id:`c2-tr-153`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`linkers`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'As well as + -ing/noun' adds information: As well as teaching, he writes novels.`,original:`Apart from being a talented painter, she also wrote poetry.`,keyword:`AS`,template:`She wrote poetry ___ a talented painter.`,solutions:[`as well as being`]},{id:`c2-tr-154`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`idioms`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Cost an arm and a leg' means be very expensive: That car cost an arm and a leg.`,original:`Building the new airport would cost an enormous amount of money.`,keyword:`ARM`,template:`Building the new airport would cost ___.`,solutions:[`an arm and a leg`]},{id:`c2-tr-155`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`collocations`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In all likelihood' means very probably: In all likelihood, she'll win.`,original:`There is a strong possibility that the strike will continue.`,keyword:`LIKELIHOOD`,template:`In all ___ continue.`,solutions:[`likelihood the strike will`,`likelihood, the strike will`,`likelihood the strike is going to`]},{id:`c2-tr-156`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Use a 'What' cleft to name the real cause: What caused the fire was a faulty cable.`,original:`The lack of funding, not the design, caused the project to collapse.`,keyword:`WHAT`,template:`___ was the lack of funding, not the design.`,solutions:[`What caused the project to collapse`,`What made the project collapse`,`What led to the project's collapse`,`What led to the collapse of the project`]},{id:`c2-tr-157`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A fronted adjective or participle can invert subject and verb in formal style: Gone is the old market square.`,original:`The days when you could travel cheaply by train are gone.`,keyword:`GONE`,template:`___ when you could travel cheaply by train.`,solutions:[`Gone are the days`]},{id:`c2-tr-158`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`conditionals`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`The third conditional describes an unreal past and its result: If he hadn't scored, we would have lost.`,original:`The team only won because their goalkeeper played brilliantly.`,keyword:`WOULD`,template:`If their goalkeeper hadn't played brilliantly, the team ___.`,solutions:[`would not have won`,`wouldn't have won`,`would have lost`]},{id:`c2-tr-159`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`inversion`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After a fronted place phrase, a simple verb can come before the subject: Into the room walked a stranger.`,original:`An elderly man was sitting under the old oak tree, feeding pigeons.`,keyword:`SAT`,template:`Under the old oak tree ___ man, feeding pigeons.`,solutions:[`sat an elderly`]},{id:`c2-tr-160`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Personal passive with a past action: subject + is believed + to have + past participle: He is believed to have left.`,original:`Critics believe that the painter destroyed many of his early works.`,keyword:`BELIEVED`,template:`The painter ___ many of his early works.`,solutions:[`is believed to have destroyed`]},{id:`c2-tr-161`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Put up with' means 'tolerate': I can't put up with the noise.`,original:`I can no longer tolerate his constant complaining.`,keyword:`PUT`,template:`I can no longer ___ his constant complaining.`,solutions:[`put up with`]},{id:`c2-tr-162`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`In formal writing, 'Should you ...' replaces 'If you ...': Should you have any questions, call us.`,original:`If you need any further information, please contact us.`,keyword:`SHOULD`,template:`___ any further information, please do not hesitate to contact us.`,solutions:[`Should you require`,`Should you need`]},{id:`c2-tr-163`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'It is vital that' use the base form (subjunctive) or 'should': It is vital that he be informed.`,original:`It's vital for every delegate to register before noon.`,keyword:`VITAL`,template:`It is ___ before noon.`,solutions:[`vital that every delegate register`,`vital that every delegate should register`,`vital for every delegate to register`]},{id:`c2-tr-164`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'On the brink of' means 'very close to': on the brink of collapse.`,original:`She was on the verge of tears when she heard the news.`,keyword:`BRINK`,template:`She was ___ when she heard the news.`,solutions:[`on the brink of tears`]},{id:`c2-tr-165`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Passive of a past deduction: must have been + past participle; the preposition stays after the verb: It must have been tampered with.`,original:`Someone must have tampered with the evidence before the trial.`,keyword:`BEEN`,template:`The evidence ___ with before the trial.`,solutions:[`must have been tampered`]},{id:`c2-tr-166`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Fall through' means 'fail to happen': The deal fell through.`,original:`The negotiations failed at the last minute.`,keyword:`FELL`,template:`The negotiations ___ at the last minute.`,solutions:[`fell through`]},{id:`c2-tr-167`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'We regret to inform you that' is the formal way to give bad news: We regret to inform you that the flight is full.`,original:`We're sorry, but your application hasn't been successful.`,keyword:`REGRET`,template:`We ___ your application has been unsuccessful.`,solutions:[`regret to inform you that`,`regret to inform you`,`regret that`]},{id:`c2-tr-168`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Recommend that' + subject + base form (no -s): She recommended that he rest.`,original:`The doctor told him he should stop smoking immediately.`,keyword:`RECOMMENDED`,template:`The doctor ___ smoking immediately.`,solutions:[`recommended that he stop`,`recommended he stop`,`recommended that he should stop`,`recommended he should stop`]},{id:`c2-tr-169`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Give/lend credence to' means 'believe or accept as true': Few experts give credence to the theory.`,original:`Nobody believed his explanation for a moment.`,keyword:`CREDENCE`,template:`Nobody ___ his explanation.`,solutions:[`gave any credence to`,`gave credence to`,`lent any credence to`,`lent credence to`,`attached any credence to`]},{id:`c2-tr-170`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Present continuous passive: is/are being + past participle: The road is being repaired.`,original:`They are renovating the old theatre at the moment.`,keyword:`BEING`,template:`The old theatre ___ at the moment.`,solutions:[`is being renovated`]},{id:`c2-tr-171`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Put off' / 'put back' mean 'postpone': The match was put off because of rain.`,original:`The organisers have postponed the conference until next spring.`,keyword:`PUT`,template:`The conference ___ until next spring.`,solutions:[`has been put off`,`has been put back`,`was put off`,`was put back`]},{id:`c2-tr-172`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal obligation: be required to + infinitive: Guests are required to sign in.`,original:`You have to show your passport at the desk.`,keyword:`REQUIRED`,template:`Passengers ___ their passports at the desk.`,solutions:[`are required to show`,`are required to present`]},{id:`c2-tr-173`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Would rather' + another subject takes the past tense with present meaning: I'd rather you stayed.`,original:`I'd prefer you not to mention this to anyone.`,keyword:`RATHER`,template:`I'd ___ this to anyone.`,solutions:[`rather you did not mention`,`rather you didn't mention`]},{id:`c2-tr-174`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A 'discrepancy between' two things is a difference that should not exist: a discrepancy between the figures.`,original:`The two reports differ significantly.`,keyword:`DISCREPANCY`,template:`There is ___ the two reports.`,solutions:[`a significant discrepancy between`,`a considerable discrepancy between`,`a marked discrepancy between`,`a major discrepancy between`]},{id:`c2-tr-175`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Present perfect passive with 'never': has never been + past participle: The record has never been broken.`,original:`Nobody has ever beaten the champion on home soil.`,keyword:`NEVER`,template:`The champion ___ on home soil.`,solutions:[`has never been beaten`]},{id:`c2-tr-176`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Mull over' means 'think about carefully': She mulled over the idea for days.`,original:`I'll think carefully about your offer and let you know tomorrow.`,keyword:`MULL`,template:`I'll ___ and let you know tomorrow.`,solutions:[`mull your offer over`,`mull over your offer`,`mull it over`]},{id:`c2-tr-177`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal notices use 'is strictly prohibited/forbidden': Parking is strictly prohibited.`,original:`Smoking isn't allowed anywhere in the building.`,keyword:`STRICTLY`,template:`Smoking ___ throughout the building.`,solutions:[`is strictly prohibited`,`is strictly forbidden`]},{id:`c2-tr-178`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Wish' + past simple expresses regret about the present: I wish I knew the answer.`,original:`It's a pity I don't speak Japanese.`,keyword:`WISH`,template:`I ___ Japanese.`,solutions:[`wish I spoke`,`wish I could speak`,`wish that I spoke`,`wish that I could speak`]},{id:`c2-tr-179`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It makes no difference to me' means 'I don't mind': It makes no difference to me when we leave.`,original:`I don't mind which restaurant we go to.`,keyword:`DIFFERENCE`,template:`It makes ___ me which restaurant we go to.`,solutions:[`no difference to`]},{id:`c2-tr-180`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After verbs like 'hate' use the passive gerund: being + past participle: I hate being kept waiting.`,original:`I hate it when people interrupt me during a presentation.`,keyword:`BEING`,template:`I hate ___ during a presentation.`,solutions:[`being interrupted`]},{id:`c2-tr-181`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Wear off' means 'gradually stop having an effect': The painkillers wore off.`,original:`The effects of the anaesthetic gradually disappeared.`,keyword:`WORE`,template:`The effects of the anaesthetic gradually ___.`,solutions:[`wore off`]},{id:`c2-tr-182`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal thanks: be grateful (to somebody) for something: We are most grateful for your support.`,original:`Thanks a lot for helping us out.`,keyword:`GRATEFUL`,template:`We are ___ your assistance.`,solutions:[`extremely grateful for`,`very grateful for`,`most grateful for`,`deeply grateful for`,`truly grateful for`,`grateful to you for`]},{id:`c2-tr-183`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Insist that' + subject + base form 'be' (subjunctive): They insisted that she be present.`,original:`The union insisted on the immediate reinstatement of the dismissed workers.`,keyword:`INSISTED`,template:`The union ___ reinstated immediately.`,solutions:[`insisted that the dismissed workers be`,`insisted the dismissed workers be`,`insisted that the dismissed workers should be`,`insisted the dismissed workers should be`]},{id:`c2-tr-184`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Stand a good chance of' + -ing means 'be likely to': He stands a good chance of getting the job.`,original:`It is very probable that she'll win the award.`,keyword:`CHANCE`,template:`She stands ___ the award.`,solutions:[`a very good chance of winning`,`a good chance of winning`,`every chance of winning`,`an excellent chance of winning`]},{id:`c2-tr-185`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Causative 'have something done' means someone else did it for us: We had the roof repaired.`,original:`We paid a technician to install the new server last week.`,keyword:`HAD`,template:`We ___ last week.`,solutions:[`had the new server installed`]},{id:`c2-tr-186`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Make up' means 'invent' (a story or excuse): He made up a story.`,original:`He invented an excuse to avoid the meeting.`,keyword:`MADE`,template:`He ___ to avoid the meeting.`,solutions:[`made up an excuse`,`made an excuse up`]},{id:`c2-tr-187`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Polite formal request: I would appreciate it if you could + infinitive: I would appreciate it if you could reply soon.`,original:`Can you send me the report by Friday?`,keyword:`APPRECIATE`,template:`I would ___ me the report by Friday.`,solutions:[`appreciate it if you could send`,`appreciate it if you would send`,`appreciate it if you sent`]},{id:`c2-tr-188`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'as if' for an unreal situation, formal English uses 'were': She talks as if she were the boss.`,original:`He behaves like the owner of the place, but he isn't.`,keyword:`WERE`,template:`He behaves as if ___ the owner of the place.`,solutions:[`he were`]},{id:`c2-tr-189`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not the slightest' + noun means 'none at all': I have not the slightest doubt.`,original:`He was not at all interested in the proposal.`,keyword:`SLIGHTEST`,template:`He had ___ in the proposal.`,solutions:[`not the slightest interest`]},{id:`c2-tr-190`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Personal passive with an ongoing action: is rumoured + to be + -ing: She is rumoured to be leaving.`,original:`Rumour has it that the company is planning a merger.`,keyword:`RUMOURED`,template:`The company ___ a merger.`,solutions:[`is rumoured to be planning`]},{id:`c2-tr-191`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Run out of' means 'have none left': We've run out of milk.`,original:`We have used up all our printer paper.`,keyword:`RUN`,template:`We have ___ printer paper.`,solutions:[`run out of`]},{id:`c2-tr-192`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal letters use 'acknowledge receipt of': We acknowledge receipt of your payment.`,original:`I got your letter, which arrived on Monday.`,keyword:`RECEIPT`,template:`I hereby acknowledge ___ letter, which arrived on Monday.`,solutions:[`receipt of your`]},{id:`c2-tr-193`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It is high time' + subject + past tense means it should already have happened: It's high time you apologised.`,original:`The government should have tackled this problem long ago.`,keyword:`HIGH`,template:`It is ___ this problem.`,solutions:[`high time the government tackled`,`high time that the government tackled`]},{id:`c2-tr-194`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Come in for' / 'come under' criticism means 'be criticised': The plan came in for fierce criticism.`,original:`The new policy was widely criticised when it was introduced.`,keyword:`CAME`,template:`The new policy ___ widespread criticism when it was introduced.`,solutions:[`came in for`,`came under`]},{id:`c2-tr-195`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Impersonal passive: It is said that + clause: It is said that he lied.`,original:`The authorities are said to have ignored the warnings.`,keyword:`IT`,template:`___ the authorities ignored the warnings.`,solutions:[`It is said that`,`It has been said that`]},{id:`c2-tr-196`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Take after' means 'resemble an older relative': He takes after his father.`,original:`She resembles her grandmother in many ways.`,keyword:`TAKES`,template:`She ___ in many ways.`,solutions:[`takes after her grandmother`]},{id:`c2-tr-197`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Wish to' is a formal alternative to 'want to': The manager wishes to see you.`,original:`The boss wants to see you right now.`,keyword:`WISHES`,template:`The director ___ with you immediately.`,solutions:[`wishes to speak`,`wishes to meet`]},{id:`c2-tr-198`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Inverted third conditional: Had it not been for + noun = If it hadn't been for: Had it not been for the map, we'd have got lost.`,original:`If it hadn't been for her help, we would have failed.`,keyword:`HAD`,template:`___ her help, we would have failed.`,solutions:[`Had it not been for`]},{id:`c2-tr-199`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Have no choice but to' + infinitive means 'be forced to': We had no choice but to wait.`,original:`We had no alternative but to accept their terms.`,keyword:`CHOICE`,template:`We ___ accept their terms.`,solutions:[`had no choice but to`,`were left with no choice but to`]},{id:`c2-tr-200`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Get something + past participle' can describe an unpleasant thing that happened to us: I got my phone stolen.`,original:`Thieves stole my bicycle from outside the library.`,keyword:`GOT`,template:`I ___ from outside the library.`,solutions:[`got my bicycle stolen`,`got my bike stolen`]},{id:`c2-tr-201`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`A 'dressing-down' is a severe reprimand: The coach gave the team a dressing-down.`,original:`The sergeant reprimanded the recruits severely for their carelessness.`,keyword:`DRESSING`,template:`The sergeant gave ___ for their carelessness.`,solutions:[`the recruits a dressing-down`,`the recruits a dressing down`,`the recruits a severe dressing-down`,`the recruits a severe dressing down`]},{id:`c2-tr-202`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal apology: We apologise for any inconvenience caused.`,original:`Sorry for any trouble this may cause.`,keyword:`APOLOGISE`,template:`We ___ inconvenience this may cause.`,solutions:[`apologise for any`]},{id:`c2-tr-203`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`The fixed subjunctive 'Be ... what it/they may' means 'whatever ... is': Be that as it may, we must act.`,original:`Whatever the consequences may be, we must tell the truth.`,keyword:`BE`,template:`___, we must tell the truth.`,solutions:[`Be the consequences what they may`]},{id:`c2-tr-204`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Have difficulty' + -ing means 'find it hard to': She has difficulty sleeping.`,original:`He finds it very hard to admit he is wrong.`,keyword:`DIFFICULTY`,template:`He ___ admitting he is wrong.`,solutions:[`has great difficulty`,`has real difficulty`,`has considerable difficulty`,`has enormous difficulty`,`has a lot of difficulty`]},{id:`c2-tr-205`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Inform somebody of something' becomes passive with the person as subject: We were informed of the change.`,original:`Nobody told us that the meeting had been cancelled.`,keyword:`INFORMED`,template:`We ___ the cancellation of the meeting.`,solutions:[`were not informed of`,`weren't informed of`,`were not informed about`,`weren't informed about`]},{id:`c2-tr-206`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Bump into' means 'meet by chance': I bumped into Ana at the shops.`,original:`I met an old classmate by chance at the airport.`,keyword:`BUMPED`,template:`I ___ an old classmate at the airport.`,solutions:[`bumped into`]},{id:`c2-tr-207`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal register prefers single verbs to phrasal verbs: 'postpone' rather than 'put off'.`,original:`We need to put the meeting off.`,keyword:`POSTPONE`,template:`It has become necessary to ___.`,solutions:[`postpone the meeting`]},{id:`c2-tr-208`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Demand that' + subject + base form (subjunctive): They demanded that she leave.`,original:`The committee demanded his immediate resignation.`,keyword:`DEMANDED`,template:`The committee ___ at once.`,solutions:[`demanded that he resign`,`demanded he resign`,`demanded that he should resign`,`demanded he should resign`]},{id:`c2-tr-209`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Pale in comparison with' means 'seem less impressive than': My efforts pale in comparison with hers.`,original:`Nothing she has written compares with her latest novel.`,keyword:`PALES`,template:`Everything else she has written ___ her latest novel.`,solutions:[`pales in comparison with`,`pales in comparison to`,`pales beside`,`pales into insignificance beside`,`pales into insignificance next to`]},{id:`c2-tr-210`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Passive of a past obligation not met: ought to have been + past participle: They ought to have been told.`,original:`Somebody ought to have warned the residents about the flood risk.`,keyword:`BEEN`,template:`The residents ___ about the flood risk.`,solutions:[`ought to have been warned`]},{id:`c2-tr-211`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Back down (on)' means 'withdraw a demand or claim': The union refused to back down.`,original:`The protesters refused to abandon their demands.`,keyword:`BACK`,template:`The protesters refused to ___ their demands.`,solutions:[`back down on`,`back away from`]},{id:`c2-tr-212`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal reminder: May we remind you that ...: May we remind you that payment is due.`,original:`Don't forget that the deadline is next Monday.`,keyword:`REMIND`,template:`May we ___ the deadline is next Monday?`,solutions:[`remind you that`,`remind you`]},{id:`c2-tr-213`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'If only' + past (formal 'were') expresses a strong wish about the present: If only I were taller!`,original:`I'd really like to be on holiday right now.`,keyword:`ONLY`,template:`If ___ on holiday right now!`,solutions:[`only I were`,`only I was`,`only I could be`]},{id:`c2-tr-214`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Play down' means 'make something seem less important': The minister played down the risks.`,original:`The manager tried to make the problem seem less serious.`,keyword:`PLAY`,template:`The manager tried to ___ the problem.`,solutions:[`play down`,`downplay`]},{id:`c2-tr-215`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Personal passive: subject + is considered (to be) + noun: He is considered a genius.`,original:`People generally consider her the finest violinist of her generation.`,keyword:`CONSIDERED`,template:`She ___ the finest violinist of her generation.`,solutions:[`is generally considered`,`is generally considered to be`,`is considered generally to be`]},{id:`c2-tr-216`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Blow over' means 'pass without lasting effect': The argument soon blew over.`,original:`The scandal eventually stopped attracting attention.`,keyword:`BLEW`,template:`The scandal eventually ___.`,solutions:[`blew over`]},{id:`c2-tr-217`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal rules often use the passive: must be accompanied by: Pets must be kept on a lead.`,original:`Kids under twelve have to be with an adult.`,keyword:`ACCOMPANIED`,template:`Children under twelve ___ by an adult.`,solutions:[`must be accompanied`,`have to be accompanied`,`are required to be accompanied`]},{id:`c2-tr-218`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Supposing' + past tense introduces an imaginary situation: Supposing you lost your job, what would you do?`,original:`Imagine that you won the lottery: what would you do?`,keyword:`SUPPOSING`,template:`___ the lottery, what would you do?`,solutions:[`Supposing you won`,`Supposing that you won`,`Supposing you were to win`,`Supposing that you were to win`]},{id:`c2-tr-219`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'There's no point (in)' + -ing means 'it's useless to': There's no point in waiting.`,original:`It's not worth arguing with him.`,keyword:`POINT`,template:`There's ___ with him.`,solutions:[`no point arguing`,`no point in arguing`]},{id:`c2-tr-220`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Under review' is a passive-meaning phrase: being reviewed: The policy is under review.`,original:`The committee is going to review the proposal next month.`,keyword:`UNDER`,template:`The proposal ___ next month.`,solutions:[`is going to be under review`,`will be under review`,`will come under review`,`is going to come under review`]},{id:`c2-tr-221`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Make out' means 'manage to understand or see': I couldn't make out the sign.`,original:`I couldn't understand what the lecturer was trying to say.`,keyword:`MAKE`,template:`I couldn't ___ the lecturer was trying to say.`,solutions:[`make out what`]},{id:`c2-tr-222`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal: keep somebody informed of something: We will keep you informed of any changes.`,original:`We'll let you know what happens.`,keyword:`INFORMED`,template:`You will ___ developments.`,solutions:[`be kept informed of`,`be kept informed about`,`be informed of`,`be informed about`]},{id:`c2-tr-223`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'It is essential that' use the base form 'be' or 'should be': It is essential that the door be locked.`,original:`It is essential for the data to be kept confidential.`,keyword:`ESSENTIAL`,template:`It is ___ kept confidential.`,solutions:[`essential that the data be`,`essential that the data should be`,`essential for the data to be`]},{id:`c2-tr-224`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Come to light' means 'become known': New evidence has come to light.`,original:`Details of the scandal were discovered last week.`,keyword:`LIGHT`,template:`Details of the scandal ___ last week.`,solutions:[`came to light`,`were brought to light`]},{id:`c2-tr-225`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Personal passive with a past action: is thought to have + past participle: He is thought to have fled.`,original:`It is thought that the fire started in the kitchen.`,keyword:`THOUGHT`,template:`The fire ___ in the kitchen.`,solutions:[`is thought to have started`,`is thought to have broken out`]},{id:`c2-tr-226`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Take off' means 'suddenly become successful': Her career took off.`,original:`Sales of the app increased dramatically after the launch.`,keyword:`TOOK`,template:`Sales of the app ___ after the launch.`,solutions:[`took off dramatically`,`dramatically took off`,`really took off`]},{id:`c2-tr-227`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal register: an event 'is attended by' people: The lecture was attended by 200 students.`,original:`Lots of people came to the opening.`,keyword:`ATTENDED`,template:`The opening ___ by a large number of people.`,solutions:[`was attended`]},{id:`c2-tr-228`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal inverted conditional: Were + subject + to-infinitive = If you were to: Were he to call, tell him I'm out.`,original:`If you change your mind, please let us know.`,keyword:`WERE`,template:`___ change your mind, please let us know.`,solutions:[`Were you to`]},{id:`c2-tr-229`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'To be frank' introduces an honest, direct opinion: To be frank, the report is weak.`,original:`Speaking honestly, I don't like the idea.`,keyword:`FRANK`,template:`___, I don't like the idea.`,solutions:[`To be frank`,`Frankly speaking`,`To be perfectly frank`]},{id:`c2-tr-230`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`In the passive, 'make' is followed by 'to' + infinitive: They were made to wait.`,original:`They made the workers sign a confidentiality agreement.`,keyword:`MADE`,template:`The workers ___ a confidentiality agreement.`,solutions:[`were made to sign`]},{id:`c2-tr-231`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Go over' / 'go through' mean 'examine carefully': Let's go over the figures.`,original:`The lawyer examined the contract in great detail.`,keyword:`WENT`,template:`The lawyer ___ with a fine-tooth comb.`,solutions:[`went over the contract`,`went through the contract`]},{id:`c2-tr-232`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal advice: You are advised (not) to + infinitive: Visitors are advised to book early.`,original:`We think it's best if you don't travel alone.`,keyword:`ADVISED`,template:`You ___ travel alone.`,solutions:[`are advised not to`,`are strongly advised not to`,`would be advised not to`,`would be well advised not to`]},{id:`c2-tr-233`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Request that' + subject + base form (subjunctive): We requested that the rule be changed.`,original:`They asked for the meeting to be held in public.`,keyword:`REQUESTED`,template:`They ___ in public.`,solutions:[`requested that the meeting be held`,`requested the meeting be held`,`requested that the meeting should be held`]},{id:`c2-tr-234`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Beyond the pale' means 'socially unacceptable': His remarks were beyond the pale.`,original:`His behaviour at the party was absolutely unacceptable.`,keyword:`BEYOND`,template:`His behaviour at the party was ___.`,solutions:[`beyond the pale`,`completely beyond the pale`]},{id:`c2-tr-235`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Prepositional verbs keep their preposition in the passive: Nothing has been heard from them.`,original:`No one has heard from the expedition since Tuesday.`,keyword:`HEARD`,template:`Nothing ___ the expedition since Tuesday.`,solutions:[`has been heard from`,`has been heard of`]},{id:`c2-tr-236`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Hold up' means 'delay': We were held up in traffic.`,original:`The rain delayed us for an hour.`,keyword:`HELD`,template:`We ___ by the rain for an hour.`,solutions:[`were held up`]},{id:`c2-tr-237`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal: notify somebody of something: Please notify us of any delay.`,original:`Tell us if your address changes.`,keyword:`NOTIFY`,template:`Please ___ any change of address.`,solutions:[`notify us of`]},{id:`c2-tr-238`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'It is advisable that' + subject + base form, or 'It is advisable to': It is advisable that you arrive early.`,original:`I strongly advise you to read the small print.`,keyword:`ADVISABLE`,template:`It is ___ the small print.`,solutions:[`advisable that you read`,`advisable that you should read`,`advisable for you to read`,`advisable to read`]},{id:`c2-tr-239`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Change beyond recognition' means 'change so much that it is hard to recognise': The town has changed beyond recognition.`,original:`I didn't recognise her at first because she had changed so much.`,keyword:`RECOGNITION`,template:`She had changed ___.`,solutions:[`beyond recognition`,`beyond all recognition`,`out of all recognition`]},{id:`c2-tr-240`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Let' has no passive form; use 'be allowed (to)': We were not allowed in.`,original:`They didn't let journalists into the courtroom.`,keyword:`ALLOWED`,template:`Journalists ___ into the courtroom.`,solutions:[`were not allowed`,`weren't allowed`,`were not allowed to go`,`weren't allowed to go`]},{id:`c2-tr-241`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Give up (on)' means 'abandon': They gave up on the project.`,original:`The government has abandoned its plans for a new airport.`,keyword:`GIVEN`,template:`The government has ___ for a new airport.`,solutions:[`given up its plans`,`given up on its plans`,`given its plans up`]},{id:`c2-tr-242`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'With regard to' is a formal way to say 'about': With regard to your request, ...`,original:`I'm writing about the job I saw advertised.`,keyword:`REGARD`,template:`I am writing ___ the position advertised.`,solutions:[`with regard to`,`in regard to`]},{id:`c2-tr-243`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'As if' + past perfect describes an unreal past situation: He looked as if he had seen a ghost.`,original:`She talks about Paris as though she had lived there, but she never has.`,keyword:`IF`,template:`She talks about Paris ___ there.`,solutions:[`as if she had lived`,`as if she'd lived`]},{id:`c2-tr-244`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'In dire straits' means 'in a very difficult situation', often financial: The club is in dire straits.`,original:`The company is in serious financial difficulty.`,keyword:`DIRE`,template:`The company is in ___.`,solutions:[`dire financial straits`,`dire straits financially`]},{id:`c2-tr-245`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Need' + -ing or 'need to be' + past participle has a passive meaning: The car needs washing.`,original:`Someone needs to repaint the bridge before winter.`,keyword:`NEEDS`,template:`The bridge ___ before winter.`,solutions:[`needs repainting`,`needs to be repainted`]},{id:`c2-tr-246`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Own up to' means 'confess': Nobody owned up to breaking the vase.`,original:`He finally admitted that he had made a mistake.`,keyword:`OWNED`,template:`He finally ___ having made a mistake.`,solutions:[`owned up to`]},{id:`c2-tr-247`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal style often uses nouns: an increase in the price of: There has been an increase in demand.`,original:`They've put up the price of fuel again.`,keyword:`INCREASE`,template:`There has been a further ___ fuel.`,solutions:[`increase in the price of`]},{id:`c2-tr-248`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Wish' + past perfect expresses regret about the past: I wish I had listened.`,original:`It's a shame you didn't tell me earlier.`,keyword:`WISH`,template:`I ___ me earlier.`,solutions:[`wish you had told`,`wish you'd told`]},{id:`c2-tr-249`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'The consensus is that' means 'most people agree that': The consensus is that prices will rise.`,original:`Everyone agrees that she is the best candidate.`,keyword:`CONSENSUS`,template:`The ___ she is the best candidate.`,solutions:[`consensus is that`,`general consensus is that`]},{id:`c2-tr-250`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past continuous passive: was/were being + past participle: The house was being watched.`,original:`Someone was following us all the way home, I'm sure of it.`,keyword:`BEING`,template:`I'm sure we ___ all the way home.`,solutions:[`were being followed`]},{id:`c2-tr-251`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Get by (on)' means 'survive with limited money': We get by on one salary.`,original:`I don't know how they manage to survive on such a low income.`,keyword:`GET`,template:`I don't know how they ___ such a low income.`,solutions:[`get by on`,`manage to get by on`]},{id:`c2-tr-252`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal: factors contribute to a result: Stress contributed to his illness.`,original:`Lots of things caused the accident.`,keyword:`CONTRIBUTED`,template:`A number of factors ___ the accident.`,solutions:[`contributed to`]},{id:`c2-tr-253`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Require that' + subject + base form (no -s): The rules require that each guest sign in.`,original:`Every passenger has to wear a seatbelt under the regulations.`,keyword:`REQUIRE`,template:`The regulations ___ a seatbelt.`,solutions:[`require that every passenger wear`,`require that every passenger should wear`,`require every passenger to wear`]},{id:`c2-tr-254`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Make reference to' means 'mention': She made no reference to the incident.`,original:`He never mentioned that he had been married before.`,keyword:`REFERENCE`,template:`He never ___ previous marriage.`,solutions:[`made any reference to his`,`made reference to his`,`made a reference to his`]},{id:`c2-tr-255`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Causative 'have something done by somebody': She had her portrait painted by an artist.`,original:`A famous architect designed their house.`,keyword:`HAD`,template:`They ___ by a famous architect.`,solutions:[`had their house designed`]},{id:`c2-tr-256`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Put out' means 'extinguish': Please put out your cigarette.`,original:`The firefighters managed to extinguish the blaze before dawn.`,keyword:`PUT`,template:`The firefighters managed to ___ before dawn.`,solutions:[`put out the blaze`,`put the blaze out`]},{id:`c2-tr-257`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal: be (not) eligible for something: Part-time staff are eligible for a bonus.`,original:`You can't get your money back on sale items.`,keyword:`ELIGIBLE`,template:`Sale items are ___ a refund.`,solutions:[`not eligible for`]},{id:`c2-tr-258`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Inverted second conditional: Were it not for + noun = If it weren't for: Were it not for you, I'd be lost.`,original:`If it weren't for the scholarship, I couldn't afford university.`,keyword:`WERE`,template:`___ the scholarship, I couldn't afford university.`,solutions:[`Were it not for`]},{id:`c2-tr-259`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Be solely responsible for' means 'be the only person responsible': He is solely responsible for the error.`,original:`The decision to close the factory was entirely the director's.`,keyword:`SOLELY`,template:`The director ___ the decision to close the factory.`,solutions:[`was solely responsible for`]},{id:`c2-tr-260`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal passive of permission: is not permitted: Photography is not permitted inside.`,original:`We regret that passengers may not smoke anywhere on board.`,keyword:`PERMITTED`,template:`Smoking ___ anywhere on board.`,solutions:[`is not permitted`,`isn't permitted`]},{id:`c2-tr-261`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Come up with' means 'think of or produce': She came up with a clever plan.`,original:`We must find a solution to this problem soon.`,keyword:`COME`,template:`We must ___ a solution to this problem soon.`,solutions:[`come up with`]},{id:`c2-tr-262`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal letters close with 'please do not hesitate to contact me'.`,original:`Ring me if you have any problems.`,keyword:`HESITATE`,template:`Should you encounter any difficulties, please ___ me.`,solutions:[`do not hesitate to contact`,`don't hesitate to contact`,`do not hesitate to call`,`don't hesitate to call`,`do not hesitate to phone`,`don't hesitate to phone`]},{id:`c2-tr-263`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'If + subject + should happen to / happens to' adds the idea of chance: If you happen to see her, say hello.`,original:`If he calls by any chance, tell him I'm out.`,keyword:`HAPPEN`,template:`If ___, tell him I'm out.`,solutions:[`he happens to call`,`he should happen to call`,`he should happen to ring`,`he happens to ring`]},{id:`c2-tr-264`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not have the faintest idea' means 'not know at all': I haven't the faintest idea where it is.`,original:`I had absolutely no idea what he was talking about.`,keyword:`FAINTEST`,template:`I didn't have ___ what he was talking about.`,solutions:[`the faintest idea`,`the faintest notion`]},{id:`c2-tr-265`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Personal passive with a past action: is known to have + past participle: She is known to have travelled widely.`,original:`It is known that the poet spent his final years in exile.`,keyword:`KNOWN`,template:`The poet ___ his final years in exile.`,solutions:[`is known to have spent`]},{id:`c2-tr-266`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Give in to' / 'give oneself up to' mean 'surrender': The thief gave himself up to the police.`,original:`The rebels eventually surrendered to government forces.`,keyword:`GAVE`,template:`The rebels eventually ___ government forces.`,solutions:[`gave in to`,`gave themselves up to`]},{id:`c2-tr-267`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal complaints use 'be of a poor standard' rather than 'bad': The service was of a poor standard.`,original:`The hotel's food was really bad.`,keyword:`STANDARD`,template:`The food at the hotel ___.`,solutions:[`was of a very poor standard`,`was of a poor standard`,`was of an extremely poor standard`,`was of a very low standard`,`was of a low standard`]},{id:`c2-tr-268`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Propose that' + subject + base form 'be' (subjunctive): He proposed that the plan be dropped.`,original:`'Why don't we postpone the vote?' said the chairwoman.`,keyword:`PROPOSED`,template:`The chairwoman ___ postponed.`,solutions:[`proposed that the vote be`,`proposed the vote be`,`proposed that the vote should be`]},{id:`c2-tr-269`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Hinge on' means 'depend completely on': Everything hinges on the vote.`,original:`The success of the plan depends on everyone's cooperation.`,keyword:`HINGES`,template:`The success of the plan ___ cooperation.`,solutions:[`hinges on everyone's`,`hinges upon everyone's`]},{id:`c2-tr-270`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Be taken aback' / 'be taken by surprise' are passive phrases meaning 'be surprised': I was taken aback by his tone.`,original:`The judge's ruling surprised everyone in the courtroom.`,keyword:`TAKEN`,template:`Everyone in the courtroom ___ by the judge's ruling.`,solutions:[`was taken aback`,`was taken by surprise`]},{id:`c2-tr-271`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Be taken in' means 'be deceived': Don't be taken in by the advert.`,original:`I was deceived by his charming manner.`,keyword:`TAKEN`,template:`I ___ by his charming manner.`,solutions:[`was taken in`,`was completely taken in`]},{id:`c2-tr-272`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal register prefers the passive and single verbs: is being investigated rather than 'we're looking into'.`,original:`We're looking into your complaint.`,keyword:`INVESTIGATED`,template:`Your complaint ___.`,solutions:[`is being investigated`,`is currently being investigated`]},{id:`c2-tr-273`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`After 'It is important that' use the base form with 'not' before it, or 'should not': It is important that he not be told.`,original:`She mustn't find out about this; that's important.`,keyword:`IMPORTANT`,template:`It is ___ out about this.`,solutions:[`important that she not find`,`important that she should not find`,`important that she shouldn't find`,`important that she does not find`,`important that she doesn't find`,`important for her not to find`]},{id:`c2-tr-274`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Rise to fame' means 'become famous': She rose to fame in the 1990s.`,original:`The singer became famous very quickly after the televised concert.`,keyword:`ROSE`,template:`The singer ___ after the televised concert.`,solutions:[`rose to fame rapidly`,`rapidly rose to fame`,`rose rapidly to fame`,`rose to fame very quickly`,`quickly rose to fame`]},{id:`c2-tr-275`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Modal passive: should not be + past participle: The risks should not be ignored.`,original:`People should not underestimate the scale of the problem.`,keyword:`UNDERESTIMATED`,template:`The scale of the problem ___.`,solutions:[`should not be underestimated`,`shouldn't be underestimated`,`ought not to be underestimated`]},{id:`c2-tr-276`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Break up' (of a meeting or party) means 'end': The party broke up at midnight.`,original:`The meeting ended earlier than expected.`,keyword:`BROKE`,template:`The meeting ___ earlier than expected.`,solutions:[`broke up`]},{id:`c2-tr-277`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Attend' is the formal verb for 'come to/go to' an event: I am unable to attend.`,original:`I can't come to the meeting on Thursday.`,keyword:`ATTEND`,template:`I regret that I will be unable ___ on Thursday.`,solutions:[`to attend the meeting`]},{id:`c2-tr-278`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`The fixed subjunctive 'So be it' means 'I accept this, even if I don't like it'.`,original:`If that's your final decision, then I accept it.`,keyword:`BE`,template:`If that's your final decision, then so ___.`,solutions:[`be it`]},{id:`c2-tr-279`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'With great reluctance' means 'very unwillingly': He agreed with great reluctance.`,original:`She accepted the offer, but very reluctantly.`,keyword:`RELUCTANCE`,template:`She accepted the offer with ___.`,solutions:[`great reluctance`,`considerable reluctance`,`extreme reluctance`,`much reluctance`]},{id:`c2-tr-280`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Past continuous passive for an action in progress: was still being + past participle: The plan was still being drafted.`,original:`Her colleagues were still discussing the issue when she arrived.`,keyword:`DISCUSSED`,template:`The issue ___ when she arrived.`,solutions:[`was still being discussed`]},{id:`c2-tr-281`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Take up' / 'take on' a challenge means 'accept it': She took on the role.`,original:`He accepted the challenge without hesitation.`,keyword:`TOOK`,template:`He ___ without hesitation.`,solutions:[`took up the challenge`,`took the challenge up`,`took on the challenge`,`took the challenge on`]},{id:`c2-tr-282`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Prior to' + -ing is a formal way to say 'before': Prior to leaving, sign the register.`,original:`Before you go into the lab, you must put on protective clothing.`,keyword:`PRIOR`,template:`Protective clothing must be worn ___ the laboratory.`,solutions:[`prior to entering`]},{id:`c2-tr-283`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`The fixed subjunctive 'Come what may' means 'whatever happens': Come what may, we'll finish on time.`,original:`Whatever happens, I'll support you.`,keyword:`COME`,template:`___, I'll support you.`,solutions:[`Come what may`]},{id:`c2-tr-284`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Not be on speaking terms with' means 'refuse to talk to somebody': They haven't been on speaking terms for years.`,original:`The two sisters don't talk to each other at all.`,keyword:`TERMS`,template:`The two sisters are ___ each other.`,solutions:[`not on speaking terms with`]},{id:`c2-tr-285`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Be supposed to' can mean 'people say': The film is supposed to be excellent.`,original:`They say the treasure lies at the bottom of the lake.`,keyword:`SUPPOSED`,template:`The treasure ___ at the bottom of the lake.`,solutions:[`is supposed to lie`,`is supposed to be`]},{id:`c2-tr-286`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Find out' means 'discover a fact': We found out the truth.`,original:`The investigators are trying to discover the cause of the crash.`,keyword:`FIND`,template:`The investigators are trying to ___ the crash.`,solutions:[`find out what caused`,`find out the cause of`]},{id:`c2-tr-287`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'As a consequence of' is a formal alternative to 'because of': As a consequence of the strike, trains were cancelled.`,original:`Because of the bad weather, the match has been called off.`,keyword:`CONSEQUENCE`,template:`___ the adverse weather, the match has been cancelled.`,solutions:[`As a consequence of`,`In consequence of`]},{id:`c2-tr-288`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Would sooner' + another subject takes the past tense with present meaning: I'd sooner you didn't smoke.`,original:`I'd prefer it if we left early.`,keyword:`SOONER`,template:`I'd ___ early.`,solutions:[`sooner we left`,`sooner that we left`]},{id:`c2-tr-289`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Factor in' means 'include something when calculating': Don't forget to factor in delays.`,original:`We need to include the cost of transport in our calculations as well.`,keyword:`FACTOR`,template:`We also need to ___ transport.`,solutions:[`factor in the cost of`,`factor in the costs of`]},{id:`c2-tr-290`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Future perfect passive: will have been + past participle: The work will have been finished by May.`,original:`The council will have completed the new library by next spring.`,keyword:`COMPLETED`,template:`The new library ___ by next spring.`,solutions:[`will have been completed`]},{id:`c2-tr-291`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Bring up' means 'raise a child': I was brought up in the country.`,original:`She was raised by her aunt in a small coastal town.`,keyword:`BROUGHT`,template:`She ___ by her aunt in a small coastal town.`,solutions:[`was brought up`]},{id:`c2-tr-292`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Laws and rules 'come into effect' or 'take effect' (formal): The ban takes effect today.`,original:`The new rules start on 1 January.`,keyword:`EFFECT`,template:`The new regulations ___ on 1 January.`,solutions:[`come into effect`,`will come into effect`,`take effect`,`will take effect`]},{id:`c2-tr-293`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Order that' + subject + base form 'be' (subjunctive): The judge ordered that he be released.`,original:`The board gave orders for all records to be destroyed.`,keyword:`ORDERED`,template:`The board ___ destroyed.`,solutions:[`ordered that all records be`,`ordered all records be`,`ordered that all records should be`,`ordered all records to be`]},{id:`c2-tr-294`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Gravity' means 'seriousness': the gravity of the crisis.`,original:`Few people realise how serious the situation is.`,keyword:`GRAVITY`,template:`Few people appreciate ___ the situation.`,solutions:[`the gravity of`]},{id:`c2-tr-295`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Has yet to be' + past participle means 'has not been ... so far': The winner has yet to be announced.`,original:`Nobody has yet found a cure for the disease.`,keyword:`FOUND`,template:`A cure for the disease ___.`,solutions:[`has yet to be found`,`has not yet been found`,`hasn't yet been found`,`has not been found yet`,`hasn't been found yet`]},{id:`c2-tr-296`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`phrasal-verbs`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Pull down' means 'demolish': They pulled down the old cinema.`,original:`The old factory was demolished to make way for flats.`,keyword:`PULLED`,template:`The old factory ___ to make way for flats.`,solutions:[`was pulled down`]},{id:`c2-tr-297`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`register`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Formal invitations use 'request the pleasure of your company'.`,original:`We'd love you to come to the opening of our new gallery.`,keyword:`PLEASURE`,template:`We request ___ at the opening of our new gallery.`,solutions:[`the pleasure of your company`,`the pleasure of your presence`]},{id:`c2-tr-298`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`subjunctive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Stipulate that' + subject + base form (subjunctive) is typical of legal style: The contract stipulates that payment be made monthly.`,original:`Under the law, tenants must be given a month's notice.`,keyword:`STIPULATES`,template:`The law ___ a month's notice.`,solutions:[`stipulates that tenants be given`,`stipulates that tenants should be given`,`stipulates that tenants must be given`]},{id:`c2-tr-299`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`vocabulary`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`'Whatsoever' after a negative noun phrase means 'at all': There is no evidence whatsoever.`,original:`She had no intention of apologising at all.`,keyword:`WHATSOEVER`,template:`She had ___ of apologising.`,solutions:[`no intention whatsoever`]},{id:`c2-tr-300`,type:`transform`,level:`C2`,topic:`passive`,prompt:`Complete the second sentence so that it means the same as the first. Use the word given.`,explanation:`Personal passive with a state: is estimated + to be: The crowd was estimated to be huge.`,original:`Experts estimate that the statue is over three thousand years old.`,keyword:`ESTIMATED`,template:`The statue ___ over three thousand years old.`,solutions:[`is estimated to be`]}]};export{e as default};